{"name":"Sea Wave","hex":"#83d6d4","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"sea-wave","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Alternative form of seawave."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/sea-wave"}],"entries":[{"title":"Wind wave","blocks":[{"text":"In fluid dynamics, a wind wave, or wind-generated water wave, is a surface wave that occurs on the free surface of bodies of water as a result of the wind blowing over the water's surface. The contact distance in the direction of the wind is known as the fetch. Waves in the oceans can travel thousands of kilometers before reaching land. Wind waves on Earth range in size from small ripples to waves over 30 m (100 ft) high, being limited by wind speed, duration, fetch, and water depth."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/04/Porto_Covo_Outubro_2014-3.jpg/330px-Porto_Covo_Outubro_2014-3.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wind_wave"},{"title":"Sea","blocks":[{"text":"A sea is a very large body of salty water. The term has two distinct meanings. The first is the interconnected body of seawater that spans most of Earth, known as the sea or the ocean. The second is a named body of water: usually either a marginal sea, or a large, nearly landlocked body of water."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1c/Mediterranean_Sea_16.61811E_38.99124N.jpg/330px-Mediterranean_Sea_16.61811E_38.99124N.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sea"},{"title":"Wave","blocks":[{"text":"In mathematics and physical science, a wave is a propagating dynamic disturbance of one or more quantities. Periodic waves oscillate repeatedly about an equilibrium (resting) value at some frequency. When the entire waveform moves in one direction, it is said to be a traveling wave; by contrast, a pair of identical superimposed periodic waves traveling in opposite directions makes a standing wave. In a standing wave, the amplitude of vibration has nulls at some positions where the wave amplitude appears smaller or even zero."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/43/2006-01-14_Surface_waves.jpg/330px-2006-01-14_Surface_waves.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wave"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4b/Sjyang_waveGeneration.png/500px-Sjyang_waveGeneration.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4b/Sjyang_waveGeneration.png/1280px-Sjyang_waveGeneration.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4b/Sjyang_waveGeneration.png/500px-Sjyang_waveGeneration.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Wave formation","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASjyang_waveGeneration.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4a/Deep_water_wave.gif/330px-Deep_water_wave.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4a/Deep_water_wave.gif/330px-Deep_water_wave.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Water particle motion of a deep water wave","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ADeep_water_wave.gif"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3d/Ocean_wave_phases_numbered.png/500px-Ocean_wave_phases_numbered.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3d/Ocean_wave_phases_numbered.png/1280px-Ocean_wave_phases_numbered.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3d/Ocean_wave_phases_numbered.png/500px-Ocean_wave_phases_numbered.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The phases of an ocean surface wave: 1. Wave Crest, where the water masses of the surface layer are moving horizontally in the same direction as the propagating wavefront. 2. Falling wave. 3. Trough, where the water masses of the surface layer are moving horizontally in the opposite direction of the wavefront direction. 4. Rising wave.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AOcean_wave_phases_numbered.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/63/Wea00810.jpg/500px-Wea00810.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/63/Wea00810.jpg/500px-Wea00810.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"NOAA ship Delaware II in bad weather on Georges Bank","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWea00810.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c7/Porto_Covo_pano_April_2009-4.jpg/500px-Porto_Covo_pano_April_2009-4.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c7/Porto_Covo_pano_April_2009-4.jpg/1280px-Porto_Covo_pano_April_2009-4.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c7/Porto_Covo_pano_April_2009-4.jpg/500px-Porto_Covo_pano_April_2009-4.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Surf on a rocky irregular bottom. Porto Covo, west coast of Portugal","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APorto_Covo_pano_April_2009-4.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7d/Ripples_Hierkonpolku_Reila.